course: dictionary
lesson: 1199
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Buck is one of them. The sentence around it tells you which meaning to use. «It cost five bucks» is about money, so buck means a dollar here. Look at the other words to find the clue.
explain: Buck can also mean a male deer, rabbit or hare, or a horse that jumps with its back arched. Nature and animal words give the clue. If the sentence talks about a field, a forest or a horse, think about animals first.
explain: Some uses of buck are phrases, and you cannot guess them from one word. Pass the buck means to make someone else responsible. Buck up means cheer up or hurry up. When a phrase does not fit the sentence, check the words around it or use a dictionary.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Buck can mean a dollar, a male deer, rabbit or hare, or a jump by a horse.
fact: «Bucks» in casual talk means dollars, as in «five bucks».
fact: Pass the buck means to make someone else responsible. Buck up means cheer up or hurry up.
fact: Game coins, gems and V-bucks are bought with real money, so never give your password for free ones: «Never use "generators" or give your password for free Robux, V-Bucks or coins.»
exercise: «A buck's worth of plastic for five bucks,» Dad laughed. What does bucks mean here? | Rabbits | Male deer | *Dollars | Horses jumping
exercise: In nature, what is a buck? | A dollar | A friend who cheers you up | *A male deer, rabbit or hare | A horse that kicks its back legs
exercise: If someone says «pass the buck», what do they do? | Jump like a horse | Cheer you up | *Make someone else responsible | Give you five dollars
test: «Just enter your Fortnite login and the V-bucks drop in your account,» said the man. What are V-bucks? | Real dollars from a bank | A horse jumping | *Game money bought in a game | A male deer
test: If your friend says, «Buck up, we're late!», what does she mean? | Give you a dollar | Jump like a horse | Pass the blame to someone else | *Cheer up or hurry up
exam: Which meaning of buck describes a horse that jumps with its back arched and kicks its back legs? | *To jump, with its back arched and kicking | A male rabbit | To hurry up | A dollar
